    },
    "blocks": [
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "When messages are routed to "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "@MessageMapping"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " methods, they are matched with "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "AntPathMatcher"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": ". By default, patterns are expected to use slash ("
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "/"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": ") as the separator. This is a good convention in web applications and similar to HTTP URLs. However, if you are more used to messaging conventions, you can switch to using dot ("
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "."
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": ") as the separator."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "The following example shows how to do so:"
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "include-code::./WebSocketConfiguration[tag=snippet,indent=0]"
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "After that, a controller can use a dot ("
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "."
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": ") as the separator in "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "@MessageMapping"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " methods, as the following example shows:"
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "include-code::./RedController[tag=snippet,indent=0]"
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "The client can now send a message to "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "/app/red.blue.green123"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "In the preceding example, we did not change the prefixes on the “broker relay”, because those depend entirely on the external message broker. See the STOMP documentation pages for the broker you use to see what conventions it supports for the destination header."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "The “simple broker”, on the other hand, does rely on the configured "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "PathMatcher"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": ", so, if you switch the separator, that change also applies to the broker and the way the broker matches destinations from a message to patterns in subscriptions."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      }
    ],
    "outline": null,
    "title": [
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "value": "Dots as Separators"
      }
    ]
  }
}
